376	## Model Selection
377	
378	Match the model to the task's difficulty rather than defaulting. Two rules are
379	defaults you will get wrong by omission, so they stay here: **always specify
380	the model explicitly when dispatching a subagent** — an omitted model inherits
381	your session's, usually the most capable and most expensive — and **a mid-tier
382	model is the floor for reviewers and for implementers working from prose
383	descriptions**, whatever the schedule pressure. The cheapest tier is for
384	transcription, where the task's own text carries the complete code to write,
385	and for single-file mechanical fixes. The per-role guidance, the complexity
386	signals, and the fix-loop escalation rule are in
387	[model-selection.md](model-selection.md).
388	
389	## The Task Loop
390	
391	**Batch small same-shape work.** When the plan lists several tasks that are
392	each a small, independent edit of the same kind — the same one-line fix,
393	constant change, or field addition repeated across files — do not dispatch
394	one subagent per task. Compose ONE dispatch brief listing every file and
395	its change, send the whole batch to a single subagent, and review its diff
396	as one unit. Reserve one-dispatch-per-task for work that needs its own
397	judgment, its own tests, or its own review surface.
398	A batch's effective risk tier for the per-task Codex gate is the MAX of the
399	batched tasks' declared tiers — batching low-tier tasks never manufactures
400	a gate skip.
401	
402	Everything you paste into a dispatch prompt — and everything a subagent
403	prints back — stays resident in your context for the rest of the session
404	and is re-read on every later turn. Hand artifacts over as files.
405	
406	**Waiting on subagents and long commands.** On Claude Code, wait in the foreground:
407	
408	- Dispatch implementer, task-reviewer, fix-round, re-review and final-reviewer subagents with `run_in_background: false`. Your next step always depends on the result, and a foreground call keeps your turn open, which is what an editor such as Zed shows as the session working. A background dispatch ends the turn while the subagent runs, and the editor shows the session idle.
409	- Run a covering command expected to finish within 10 minutes in the foreground, with an explicit timeout of at least its expected runtime and at most 600000 ms.
410	- A command that may run past 10 minutes is a tracked job. Allocate it in the foreground with `scripts/tracked-job new PLAN_FILE <name> --budget-ms <budget>` (the watch budget below; the helper writes the `jobdir=` line in the ledger itself), launch `scripts/tracked-job run <jobdir> -- <command>` through the tracked background option with the budget as its timeout, then watch it in the foreground with repeated `scripts/tracked-job wait <jobdir> --max-ms 600000` calls: `exited code=0` is success; any other `exited` code is a failed command, handled as that command's failure (a failed test is a fix round, a failed covering command a failed task); `running` at the bound means another `wait`; on `lost`, run `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` first and read its answer before you go on: only `stopped` or `not-running` means the survivors are swept and the job can be treated as failed (a process in the job's group that carried no fence is left alone and counted as `skipped` in `<jobdir>/meta.json`, and one that outlived the KILL is counted in `survivors`; report a nonzero `survivors` or `skipped`, or a record you cannot read, to your human partner with the jobdir and pgid before you go on, and never call that job stopped); `busy` means the lock was held and nothing was swept, so run `stop` again; `status=error` means the job directory could not be read, so escalate it to your human partner naming the jobdir and leave the job live; `lost-unswept pgid=<n>` means the sweep needs a shell that can list processes and read their identity (the sandbox cannot), so the job stays live to the stop guard until `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` runs where it can: report it to your human partner with its pgid beside the failure, and expect a reused pid to read as `running` until that sweep. When you need a job's output, write the tail of its `out.log` to `<workspace>/reports/job-<name>.txt` and cite the path; output never goes in the ledger. A background shell you start in a turn without `tracked-job` is live work no job record names: end the turn while one is still running and the stop guard refuses that turn end as `live-shell`. Codex reviews already follow this shape through the recipe's `status <job-id> --wait --json` loop.
411	- **Watch budget.** The total watch on one job is three times the plan's stated runtime for it, or 60 minutes when the plan states none, capped at the harness's maximum background lifetime (7200000 ms on Claude Code). The watch ends when `wait` reports `exited`, `lost` or `lost-unswept`; a supervisor the harness killed writes no exit file, and `wait` reports it as `lost`. When the budget is spent with the job still running, run `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` and read its answer before you end the turn: `stopped` or `not-running` means the job is over, so write the `out.log` tail to `<workspace>/reports/job-<name>.txt` and report BLOCKED naming that path (a nonzero `survivors` or `skipped` in `<jobdir>/meta.json`, or a record you cannot read, is reported the same way, with the jobdir and pgid, and that job is never called stopped); `busy` means the workspace lock was held and nothing changed, so run `stop` again; `stop-timeout` means the supervisor has not concluded the job, so run `stop` again, three calls in all, and escalate a third one to your human partner as a job still live, naming the jobdir; `status=error` means the job directory could not be read, so escalate it the same way, naming the jobdir; `lost-unswept pgid=<n>` leaves the job live, so report it to your human partner as still live with its pgid, never as stopped.
412	
413	On a harness whose subagents run asynchronously, never poll a wait interface with short timeouts, and never sit in one silent, open-ended wait either. While you have local work — ledger updates, packaging the next review, reading reports — keep working; child results arrive on their own. When you are genuinely idle, wait in bounded stretches (five to ten minutes, where your platform allows), and between stretches post one line of status and reconcile your live children: list them, and chase any that finished without reporting. A bounded stretch keeps nearly all of a long wait's efficiency while guaranteeing a stuck or lost child is noticed within minutes, not at the end of the session. Codex's `wait_agent` blocks inside the turn; `using-hyperpowers/references/codex-tools.md` maps it.
