129	## Setup
130	
131	Read before you settle: run the `sync` and `sdd-dir` steps below and read
132	the ledger's plan line, if the ledger exists (reading it changes nothing).
133	Then settle where the work happens: use hyperpowers:using-git-worktrees,
134	which detects an existing worktree or asks your human partner to choose
135	between staying on the current branch, a new branch in place, and a
136	worktree, and reports the mode. When the plan line names an integration
137	branch, this is a later session of a running plan: hand using-git-worktrees
138	that branch and your session id. Never start implementation on a main/master
139	branch unless your human partner chose "stay on the current branch" in that
140	question; that answer is the consent, and the dossier no longer depends on
141	the branch.
142	
143	Conversation memory does not survive compaction. In real sessions,
144	controllers that lost their place have re-dispatched entire completed task
145	sequences — the single most expensive failure observed. Track progress in
146	a ledger file, not only in todos.
147	
148	- Each plan owns a workspace: at skill start, run `scripts/sdd-state sync PLAN_FILE`
149	  (it fetches the repository's state branch; outcomes below), then this
150	  skill's `scripts/sdd-dir PLAN_FILE` — it prints the plan's workspace
151	  inside the repository's state checkout under the user cache
152	  (`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/hyperpowers/state/<key>/plans/<plan-slug>-<hash8>/`,
153	  one checkout per clone, shared by every worktree of it), home to every
154	  artifact for THIS plan: ledger, briefs, reports, review packages, claims,
155	  tier-skip summary. The ledger, reports, notes and claims are committed to
156	  the orphan branch `hyperpowers/state` by `scripts/sdd-state snapshot PLAN_FILE`
157	  and travel between hosts with that branch; briefs and review packages are
158	  ignored there and regenerate. The cache is off any protected path, so
159	  writing there never triggers a permission prompt, and it is outside the
160	  working tree, so it can never be committed with the code. Another plan's
161	  directory is never yours to read or write. When `sdd-dir` reports
162	  `backend=plain` on stderr, the state branch is unavailable on this host
163	  and the dossier will not travel from it: tell your human partner once and
164	  continue.
165	- `sdd-state sync` outcomes: `up-to-date`, `fast-forwarded`, `merged`,
166	  `ahead`, `created`, `absent`, `no-remote`, `assumed-absent`, `plain`:
167	  continue. `offline` (exit 1): continue on the local checkout and, as soon
168	  as the ledger exists (the next bullet), append
169	  `state: sync offline at <time>` to it. `offline-no-state` (exit 1), or
170	  `needs-sync` from `sdd-dir`: stop; this host has no dossier and cannot
171	  learn whether the remote holds one. Ask your human partner to get online,
172	  or to confirm `scripts/sdd-state sync --assume-absent PLAN_FILE` when they
173	  know no remote dossier exists. `diverged` (exit 3): stop and present both
174	  tips and the printed `merge=` command as a question in the Deciding
175	  Together shape; never merge on your own. `busy` (exit 3): retry `sync`
176	  once after the other session's writes settle, then ask.
177	  `foreign` (exit 2): the branch at the state name is not a hyperpowers
178	  state branch; stop, relay the stderr remedy (set git config
179	  `hyperpowers.stateBranch` to another name), and ask.
180	  `remote-finished plans=<ws,...>` (exit 3): another host finished a plan
181	  whose workspace on this host holds ledger lines that are not on the
182	  branch. When the list names this plan's workspace, stop, show your human
183	  partner the lines this host added (the ledger's tail beyond the last
184	  snapshot), and ask whether to snapshot them (then `sync` again, which
185	  reports `diverged` for their merge) or to discard them. A workspace in
186	  the list that is not this plan's belongs to another plan: report its
187	  name and stop without reading or changing it.
188	  Any other status, `status=error` (exit 2) among them, stops the run until
189	  `sync` succeeds; show its stderr line.
190	- Check for this plan's ledger at `<workspace>/progress.md` — this plan's
191	  workspace only, never a sibling's. The workspace is the plan's identity:
192	  `scripts/sdd-dir` maps a plan to ONE directory whatever path form you
193	  hand it, so a ledger sitting in YOUR workspace is this plan's progress
194	  even when its first line records the path in another form. Compare
195	  repo-relative paths: reduce the plan you were handed to its path relative
196	  to the plan repository's toplevel; a line holding an absolute path matches
197	  when it lies inside this repository and reduces to the same relative path,
198	  or, when it names a path that does not exist on this host (a ledger
199	  written on another machine), when it ends with that relative path. Tasks
200	  with a `Task <N>: complete` line are
201	  DONE — do not re-dispatch them; resume at the first task without one. A
202	  task whose last line is a fix round is mid-loop: resume the loop at the
203	  next round. A task whose last line is its implementer identity is a
204	  dispatch in flight: do not re-dispatch — reconcile that agent first
205	  (list your live children, chase its report), resume it by the recorded
206	  id where your harness allows, and only if it cannot be recovered
207	  dispatch a takeover, recording the takeover's identity beside the
208	  original. The scan only reads: every action it calls for (a resume, a
209	  takeover, the next task) waits until `verify` below has returned `ok`.
210	  A first line that resolves to a genuinely different plan
211	  file means this workspace is corrupt, not that you wandered into a
212	  sibling's: stop and surface it to your human partner rather than
213	  starting fresh on top of another plan's state.
214	- When there is no ledger, create it with its identity as the first line,
215	  `# SDD ledger — plan: <repo-relative plan path>` (never an absolute path:
216	  the ledger travels between hosts), through a no-clobber write so two
217	  sessions that both saw no ledger cannot overwrite each other:
218	  `( set -o noclobber; printf '# SDD ledger — plan: %s\n' <repo-relative plan path> > <workspace>/progress.md )`.
219	  When that write fails because the file now exists, another session
220	  created it first: continue as if you had found it. Whether you created
221	  it or found one, append your session line (with `>>`, never a rewrite):
222	  `session <sid>: mode <current-branch|new-branch|worktree> on <task branch or detached> integrating <integration branch> at <toplevel> started <UTC time>`,
223	  where `<sid>` is `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` on Claude Code and, on a harness
224	  without one, a value you generate once as `$(date -u +%s)-$RANDOM$RANDOM`,
225	  and the mode is what using-git-worktrees reported. Append a fresh session
226	  line whenever you move to a new task branch or detach; your newest line is
227	  your location. When your human partner assigned this session specific
228	  tasks, add `session <sid>: tasks <N,M,...>`. Then, once registered (the
229	  next bullet), run `scripts/sdd-state verify PLAN_FILE` (on a found ledger
230	  this is the check that this host holds the code it relies on): `ok` continues;
231	  anything else stops Setup until `verify` passes again. `code-missing`
232	  names commits the ledger relies on that this checkout lacks, so ask your
233	  human partner to pull the named branch; `wrong-repo` names the repository
234	  the ledger was handed off from, so ask which checkout this work belongs
235	  in; `plan-stale` names the plan repository branch to pull, and you read
236	  the plan again after that pull.
237	- Register the plan before anything else is claimed:
238	  `scripts/sdd-state register PLAN_FILE --session <sid> --integration <branch>`,
239	  where `<branch>` is the branch the branch choice produced (the plan's
240	  integration branch; later sessions read it from the ledger's plan line and
241	  pass the same). `registered` continues; `wrong-branch` means this plan is
242	  already running on another integration branch: switch to it or ask;
243	  `spec-branch-mismatch` means a sibling plan of this spec runs on another
244	  branch: use that branch; `branch-closing` means the plan's branch is being
245	  merged and deleted: wait for your human partner; `detached-head` (exit 11)
246	  means a first registration found the repository the line names on a
247	  detached HEAD, as a host-managed workspace can be, and the plan ref is
248	  named after a branch: ask your human partner for the branch to create
249	  (the integration branch itself when plan and code share one repository),
250	  create it in place with `git -C <the repository the line names> switch -c <name>`,
251	  and register again. Any other status stops Setup until `register`
252	  succeeds; show its stderr line. A later session of a
253	  running plan starts its worktree detached at the integration head
254	  (using-git-worktrees says how) and never checks the integration branch
255	  out; a later session (the ledger already has a plan line naming the
256	  integration branch) that is not already detached at that branch's head
257	  runs `git switch --detach <branch>` before `register`, whatever mode
258	  using-git-worktrees reported (an existing worktree, a native tool's
259	  worktree, or work in place); a worktree that skill created detached is
260	  already there. Before that detach, and before any claim, a later session
261	  compares `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` with the `at <toplevel>` of every
262	  other session's newest session line: a match means you are inside another
263	  session's worktree (a native tool can resume one under the same name), so
264	  do not detach, switch or claim there. Leave it, create your own worktree
265	  with a name that carries your session id (using-git-worktrees, Detaching a
266	  later session), append a fresh session line from it, and detach that one.
267	  When the plan file lives in another repository
268	  than the code, pass `--code-repo <code toplevel>` to every `sdd-state`
269	  call; an omitted option means the current repository, and a wrong one is
270	  `wrong-repo`.
271	- Quote the graph in the ledger: run `scripts/sdd-state deps PLAN_FILE` and
272	  append its lines after the scan table. The scan's conflict rows include
273	  `scripts/sdd-state deps --check PLAN_FILE --ledger <workspace>/progress.md`'s
274	  lines: each `conflict tasks=A,B ...` is a row for your human partner, settled
275	  by an amendment (`scripts/sdd-state amend PLAN_FILE --from <edited copy>
276	  --base <plan= blob> --message <subject>`, with `--accept 'tasks=A,B path=<p>'`
277	  when they accept the overlap; the edited copy is a file outside the
278	  checkout's plan path, a copy in the plan's workspace, never the checked-out
279	  plan file itself, which `amend` refuses) and never by editing the plan in
280	  the worktree alone, because the plan ref is what every session reads.
281	- The ledger is your recovery map: the commits it names exist in git even
282	  when your context no longer remembers creating them. After compaction,
283	  trust the ledger and `git log` over your own recollection.
284	- The workspace is scratch for the working tree and a record on the branch:
285	  the Finish step removes the plan's directory through
286	  `scripts/sdd-state finish PLAN_FILE --session <sid>`, and the branch history keeps it, so
287	  an evidence note can cite `git show <sha>:plans/<ws>/<file>`. Nothing
288	  inside the checkout is reclaimed automatically; `scripts/sdd-state prune --repo <dir>`
289	  lists and removes idle plan directories only when asked. Snapshot the
290	  workspace (`scripts/sdd-state snapshot PLAN_FILE`) after the pre-flight
291	  scan table and before the final review; `scripts/sdd-state complete`
292	  snapshots after every completion line it writes, so a hand-off or a crash
293	  loses at most the task in flight. The completion's durable record is the
294	  snapshot `complete` takes, so that helper's status line decides whether
295	  you go on: `status=complete` continues, `busy` and `error` are retried,
296	  and every other status stops. A snapshot you run by hand answers
297	  `snapshot` (or `plain`, where no state branch exists), which continues;
298	  `none` means what you just wrote is not in the workspace the helper sees,
299	  and `error` (exit 2) means the commit or the ref update failed; both stop
300	  until the snapshot succeeds.
301	  When your human partner asks to
302	  hand the run to another host, run `scripts/sdd-state handoff PLAN_FILE --push`
303	  (the only time the state branch is pushed, never on your own initiative),
304	  report its status line, relay `plan-not-committed` with the commands to
305	  commit the plan, relay `code-dirty` with the listed paths as a question
306	  (only an explicit yes passes `--code-dirty-ok`), relay `detached-head`
307	  by asking for a branch name and creating it in the repository the line
308	  names (`repo=code` or `repo=plan`: `git -C <that repository> switch -c <name>`)
309	  before retrying, relay `shallow-source` (exit 12) the same way (a full
310	  clone, or `git -C <that repository> fetch --unshallow`, since a shallow
311	  code or plan repository cannot record a root), and stop on `plain`
312	  (exit 7: this host has no state branch, so there is no dossier to push),
313	  `scan-failed` (exit 5: the pre-push scan named in `hyperpowers.prePushScan`
314	  rejected the range; show its output and ask) and `push-failed` (exit 1:
315	  the remote moved or could not be reached; run `sync` first, then retry):
316	  none of them is a hand-off, so report nothing as ready to pull.
317	  `snapshot ... push=none` (exit 0) is no hand-off either: the plan
318	  repository has no remote, so the record is committed on this host and
319	  nothing left it; report it as a local snapshot and ask your human partner
320	  for a remote if the dossier has to travel. On
321	  `pushed`, list the branches named in `code=` and, when present, `plan=`
322	  for your human partner to push here and pull there. You push none of
323	  them unless asked.
324	
325	Read the plan once, note its context and Global Constraints, and create a
326	todo per task where your harness surfaces todos — the ledger is the
327	progress record either way; todos mirror it, never replace it. Read the
328	plan's `**Spec:**` header and the spec file it names: the spec is the
329	binding authority the plan argues from, and conflicts inside the plan
330	resolve against it. A plan whose spec is `none` or unreachable gets a
331	ledger line saying so — without a spec, a conflict has no tiebreaker but
332	your human partner.
333	
334	When the plan has a spec, dry-run the decision compiler over it now:
335	`bash <finishing skill's directory>/scripts/decisions-compile <spec> --dry-run`
336	(the finishing-a-development-branch skill, `../finishing-a-development-branch/`
337	relative to this one). Exit 0 confirms the spec's `## Decisions` list parses;
338	its numbered listing goes into the ledger under a `Decisions listing:` line
339	for the extractor's dispatch. Exit 2 for a missing list is the legacy-spec
340	case: draft the list from the spec's own text, one item per decision it
341	states, show it to your human partner, and write it into the spec once they
342	approve it, committing that edit on the branch. Any other exit 2 names what
343	to fix first (a spec that is not committed, or a `Supersedes:` problem) to
344	resolve with them the same way. After any repair, run the dry run again
345	until it exits 0, and only then write its numbered listing into the ledger.
346	The session is at its start, so this costs no cycle time. When the plan has
347	no spec (`none`, or unreachable, per the ledger line above), write
348	`Decisions: none (no spec)` in the ledger and skip the listing, the
349	extractor and the spec in the hand-off: finishing then takes its test-only
350	path.
351	
352	Before dispatching Task 1, scan the plan once for conflicts, writing down
353	what you checked as you check it:
354	
355	- tasks that contradict each other or the plan's Global Constraints
356	- anything the plan explicitly mandates that the review rubric treats as a
357	  defect (a test that asserts nothing, verbatim duplication of a logic block)
358	
359	The scan's output is a table, not a verdict. One row for every pair of tasks
360	that share a file or an interface: the two tasks, what one produces against
361	what the other consumes, and what you found. One row for every task: whether
362	its own text agrees with itself — the tests it specifies against the code it
363	specifies, the files it creates against the files it later touches. "The scan
364	is clean" without those rows is not a scan you ran.
365	
366	Write the table to the ledger. Then present everything it surfaced to your
367	human partner as ONE batched question — each finding beside the plan text
368	that mandates it, asking which governs — before execution begins, not one
369	interrupt per discovery mid-plan. Each finding is a decision with
370	consequences downstream, so it gets the shape the brainstorming skill's
371	Deciding Together section describes: the conflict and what each resolution
372	costs in chat, then the selection widget to collect the answers. If the scan
373	is clean, proceed without comment. The review loop remains the net for
374	conflicts that only emerge from implementation.
375	
376	## Model Selection
377	
378	Match the model to the task's difficulty rather than defaulting. Two rules are
379	defaults you will get wrong by omission, so they stay here: **always specify
380	the model explicitly when dispatching a subagent** — an omitted model inherits
381	your session's, usually the most capable and most expensive — and **a mid-tier
382	model is the floor for reviewers and for implementers working from prose
383	descriptions**, whatever the schedule pressure. The cheapest tier is for
384	transcription, where the task's own text carries the complete code to write,
385	and for single-file mechanical fixes. The per-role guidance, the complexity
386	signals, and the fix-loop escalation rule are in
387	[model-selection.md](model-selection.md).
388	
389	## The Task Loop
390	
391	**Batch small same-shape work.** When the plan lists several tasks that are
392	each a small, independent edit of the same kind — the same one-line fix,
393	constant change, or field addition repeated across files — do not dispatch
394	one subagent per task. Compose ONE dispatch brief listing every file and
395	its change, send the whole batch to a single subagent, and review its diff
396	as one unit. Reserve one-dispatch-per-task for work that needs its own
397	judgment, its own tests, or its own review surface.
398	A batch's effective risk tier for the per-task Codex gate is the MAX of the
399	batched tasks' declared tiers — batching low-tier tasks never manufactures
400	a gate skip.
401	
402	Everything you paste into a dispatch prompt — and everything a subagent
403	prints back — stays resident in your context for the rest of the session
404	and is re-read on every later turn. Hand artifacts over as files.
405	
406	**Waiting on subagents and long commands.** On Claude Code, wait in the foreground:
407	
408	- Dispatch implementer, task-reviewer, fix-round, re-review and final-reviewer subagents with `run_in_background: false`. Your next step always depends on the result, and a foreground call keeps your turn open, which is what an editor such as Zed shows as the session working. A background dispatch ends the turn while the subagent runs, and the editor shows the session idle.
409	- Run a covering command expected to finish within 10 minutes in the foreground, with an explicit timeout of at least its expected runtime and at most 600000 ms.
410	- A command that may run past 10 minutes is a tracked job. Allocate it in the foreground with `scripts/tracked-job new PLAN_FILE <name> --budget-ms <budget>` (the watch budget below; the helper writes the `jobdir=` line in the ledger itself), launch `scripts/tracked-job run <jobdir> -- <command>` through the tracked background option with the budget as its timeout, then watch it in the foreground with repeated `scripts/tracked-job wait <jobdir> --max-ms 600000` calls: `exited code=0` is success; any other `exited` code is a failed command, handled as that command's failure (a failed test is a fix round, a failed covering command a failed task); `running` at the bound means another `wait`; on `lost`, run `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` first and read its answer before you go on: only `stopped` or `not-running` means the survivors are swept and the job can be treated as failed (a process in the job's group that carried no fence is left alone and counted as `skipped` in `<jobdir>/meta.json`, and one that outlived the KILL is counted in `survivors`; report a nonzero `survivors` or `skipped`, or a record you cannot read, to your human partner with the jobdir and pgid before you go on, and never call that job stopped); `busy` means the lock was held and nothing was swept, so run `stop` again; `status=error` means the job directory could not be read, so escalate it to your human partner naming the jobdir and leave the job live; `lost-unswept pgid=<n>` means the sweep needs a shell that can list processes and read their identity (the sandbox cannot), so the job stays live to the stop guard until `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` runs where it can: report it to your human partner with its pgid beside the failure, and expect a reused pid to read as `running` until that sweep. When you need a job's output, write the tail of its `out.log` to `<workspace>/reports/job-<name>.txt` and cite the path; output never goes in the ledger. A background shell you start in a turn without `tracked-job` is live work no job record names: end the turn while one is still running and the stop guard refuses that turn end as `live-shell`. Codex reviews already follow this shape through the recipe's `status <job-id> --wait --json` loop.
411	- **Watch budget.** The total watch on one job is three times the plan's stated runtime for it, or 60 minutes when the plan states none, capped at the harness's maximum background lifetime (7200000 ms on Claude Code). The watch ends when `wait` reports `exited`, `lost` or `lost-unswept`; a supervisor the harness killed writes no exit file, and `wait` reports it as `lost`. When the budget is spent with the job still running, run `tracked-job stop <jobdir>` and read its answer before you end the turn: `stopped` or `not-running` means the job is over, so write the `out.log` tail to `<workspace>/reports/job-<name>.txt` and report BLOCKED naming that path (a nonzero `survivors` or `skipped` in `<jobdir>/meta.json`, or a record you cannot read, is reported the same way, with the jobdir and pgid, and that job is never called stopped); `busy` means the workspace lock was held and nothing changed, so run `stop` again; `stop-timeout` means the supervisor has not concluded the job, so run `stop` again, three calls in all, and escalate a third one to your human partner as a job still live, naming the jobdir; `status=error` means the job directory could not be read, so escalate it the same way, naming the jobdir; `lost-unswept pgid=<n>` leaves the job live, so report it to your human partner as still live with its pgid, never as stopped.
412	
413	On a harness whose subagents run asynchronously, never poll a wait interface with short timeouts, and never sit in one silent, open-ended wait either. While you have local work — ledger updates, packaging the next review, reading reports — keep working; child results arrive on their own. When you are genuinely idle, wait in bounded stretches (five to ten minutes, where your platform allows), and between stretches post one line of status and reconcile your live children: list them, and chase any that finished without reporting. A bounded stretch keeps nearly all of a long wait's efficiency while guaranteeing a stuck or lost child is noticed within minutes, not at the end of the session. Codex's `wait_agent` blocks inside the turn; `using-hyperpowers/references/codex-tools.md` maps it.
414	
415	### 1. Dispatch the implementer
416	
417	Record BASE right after `git switch <branch>` and before dispatching, as the
418	task branch's fork point from the integration branch:
419	`git merge-base HEAD refs/heads/<integration branch>` (the full ref name, so
420	a tag with the branch's name cannot be read instead). On a fresh claim that
421	is the claim's `from=` commit. On a takeover it is the original claim's base,
422	or, once the conflict route has merged the integration branch into the task
423	branch, the integration head merged in; the previous holder's commits stay in
424	the review package and the fix-round diffs either way, which both need BASE.
425	A HEAD read before the claim can lag the integration branch while other
426	sessions merge.
427	
428	- **Task brief:** before dispatching an implementer, run this skill's
429	  `scripts/task-brief PLAN_FILE N` — it extracts the task's full text to a
430	  uniquely named file and prints `wrote <path>: <N> lines`. Take the path
431	  from that line; do not paste the line itself into a dispatch. Compose the
432	  dispatch so the
433	  brief stays the single source of
434	  requirements. Your dispatch should contain: (1) one line on where this
435	  task fits in the project; (2) the brief path, introduced as "read this
436	  first — it is your requirements, with the exact values to use verbatim";
437	  (3) interfaces and decisions from earlier tasks that the brief cannot
438	  know; (4) your resolution of any ambiguity you noticed in the brief;
439	  (5) the report-file path and report contract. Exact values (numbers,
440	  magic strings, signatures, test cases) appear only in the brief. Never
441	  make a subagent read the whole plan file.
442	- **Report file:** name the implementer's report file after the brief
443	  (brief `…/task-N-brief.md` → report `…/task-N-report.md`) and put it in
444	  the dispatch prompt. The implementer writes the full report there and
445	  returns only status, commits, a one-line test summary, and concerns.
446	- A dispatch prompt describes one task, not the session's history. Do not
447	  paste accumulated prior-task summaries ("state after Tasks 1-3") into
448	  later dispatches — a real session's dispatch hit 42k chars of which 99%
449	  was pasted history. A fresh subagent needs its task, the interfaces it
450	  touches, and the global constraints. Nothing else.
451	- The dispatch carries the no-subagents contract (it is in the
452	  implementer template): the implementer never dispatches subagents —
453	  not helpers, and never a reviewer. Review arrives from you, after the
454	  report. In real sessions, every reviewer a worker spawned duplicated
455	  the task review the controller dispatched anyway — a full extra
456	  review seat per task.
457	- Subagents follow hyperpowers:test-driven-development for the task's tests.
458	- If an earlier task deferred a finding in the area this task touches — a
459	  Minor in the ledger, or one your human partner decided — carry a pointer
460	  to that ledger entry in the dispatch.
461	- Ask what to take: `scripts/sdd-state next PLAN_FILE --session <sid>`.
462	  `status=next tasks=<list>` names the claimable tasks in plan order, this
463	  session's own claims first; claim the first:
464	  `scripts/sdd-state claim PLAN_FILE N --session <sid>`. `claimed` prints the
465	  task branch and its base: check the branch out (`git switch <branch>`) and
466	  dispatch on it; `held` with your own session id (`own=1`, a resume after
467	  compaction) prints the same two fields: check the branch out and proceed;
468	  `held` with `own=1` and `merged=1` means the task is already merged and its
469	  branch deleted: check nothing out and resume it at its completion line
470	  (Complete the task, below); `claimed` with `takeover=1` and `merged=1`
471	  means the same after a takeover: the task is already merged, so check
472	  nothing out, dispatch nothing, and resume it at its completion line (the
473	  previous holder's task branch may still exist; you do not need it);
474	  `held` from `claim` after `next` offered the task is a race lost, run
475	  `next` again; `blocked` means the plan moved under you, run `next` again.
476	  A task held by another session is ordinary while next offers another: take
477	  what it offers. When `next` prints `status=none reason=<reason>`:
478	  `complete` goes to the final train (Finish below); `assigned-complete` is
479	  reported and the session hands back; `held` and `blocked` mean post a
480	  waiting record (`scripts/sdd-state wait PLAN_FILE --session <sid> --kind
481	  tasks --note "<the held= and waiting= fields>"`), print
482	  `scripts/sdd-state board PLAN_FILE`, and hand back with the board's session
483	  lines in your message. Take a held task over only on your human partner's
484	  explicit yes to a question that names the holder and the time since
485	  (`--takeover`), recording the takeover beside the original identity;
486	  `own-blocked` means an amendment put a prerequisite under your own claim:
487	  post, print the board, hand back, and let your human partner remove the
488	  edge or let the prerequisite complete. `done`, `no-workspace` and `error`
489	  keep their handling: `done`: another session completed the task since you
490	  read the ledger; read its completion line, mark the task done, and never
491	  dispatch it. `no-workspace` (exit 2): the workspace is gone (finished or
492	  pruned elsewhere); stop, run `sync`, and re-read the ledger before
493	  anything else. `error` (exit 2, the same exit as `no-workspace` and a
494	  different problem): a read or a write failed, and the claim may already
495	  have been published; only your own retry of the same claim (which then
496	  answers `held own=1`) or a takeover clears it. Stop, show the stderr line,
497	  and retry the same claim once its cause is fixed; `sync` is still not the
498	  answer. Any other status stops the dispatch until `claim` succeeds; show
499	  its stderr line.
500	- Record the implementer's agent identity from the dispatch result in the
501	  ledger's task entry
502	  (`Task <N>: implementer <agent-id-or-name> (model <model>, session <sid>, worktree <path>, branch <name>)`) —
503	  fix-loop rounds 1-3 resume this agent where the harness has no foreground
504	  dispatch and dispatch on its recorded model where it does, and after compaction the ledger is
505	  the only place the identity survives once its agent has exited. An
506	  identity that was never written down forces a fresh takeover where a
507	  resume was owed.
508	- When another session may be live on this plan, append ledger lines with a
509	  quoted heredoc (`cat >> <workspace>/progress.md <<'EOF'`), never with the
510	  Edit tool, whose read-modify-write drops a line another session appended
511	  in between.
512	- One implementer at a time in this session: parallel work is other sessions' (one controller per task branch); never dispatch two implementers at once here.
513	- Where the Agent tool offers `run_in_background`, dispatch with it false; where it does not (fork mode in an interactive terminal), dispatches run in the background and the stop guard treats them as live work.
