129	## Setup
130	
131	Read before you settle: run the `sync` and `sdd-dir` steps below and read
132	the ledger's plan line, if the ledger exists (reading it changes nothing).
133	Then settle where the work happens: use hyperpowers:using-git-worktrees,
134	which detects an existing worktree or asks your human partner to choose
135	between staying on the current branch, a new branch in place, and a
136	worktree, and reports the mode. When the plan line names an integration
137	branch, this is a later session of a running plan: hand using-git-worktrees
138	that branch and your session id. Never start implementation on a main/master
139	branch unless your human partner chose "stay on the current branch" in that
140	question; that answer is the consent, and the dossier no longer depends on
141	the branch.
142	
143	Conversation memory does not survive compaction. In real sessions,
144	controllers that lost their place have re-dispatched entire completed task
145	sequences — the single most expensive failure observed. Track progress in
146	a ledger file, not only in todos.
147	
148	- Each plan owns a workspace: at skill start, run `scripts/sdd-state sync PLAN_FILE`
149	  (it fetches the repository's state branch; outcomes below), then this
150	  skill's `scripts/sdd-dir PLAN_FILE` — it prints the plan's workspace
151	  inside the repository's state checkout under the user cache
152	  (`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/hyperpowers/state/<key>/plans/<plan-slug>-<hash8>/`,
153	  one checkout per clone, shared by every worktree of it), home to every
154	  artifact for THIS plan: ledger, briefs, reports, review packages, claims,
155	  tier-skip summary. The ledger, reports, notes and claims are committed to
156	  the orphan branch `hyperpowers/state` by `scripts/sdd-state snapshot PLAN_FILE`
157	  and travel between hosts with that branch; briefs and review packages are
158	  ignored there and regenerate. The cache is off any protected path, so
159	  writing there never triggers a permission prompt, and it is outside the
160	  working tree, so it can never be committed with the code. Another plan's
161	  directory is never yours to read or write. When `sdd-dir` reports
162	  `backend=plain` on stderr, the state branch is unavailable on this host
163	  and the dossier will not travel from it: tell your human partner once and
164	  continue.
165	- `sdd-state sync` outcomes: `up-to-date`, `fast-forwarded`, `merged`,
166	  `ahead`, `created`, `absent`, `no-remote`, `assumed-absent`, `plain`:
167	  continue. `offline` (exit 1): continue on the local checkout and, as soon
168	  as the ledger exists (the next bullet), append
169	  `state: sync offline at <time>` to it. `offline-no-state` (exit 1), or
170	  `needs-sync` from `sdd-dir`: stop; this host has no dossier and cannot
171	  learn whether the remote holds one. Ask your human partner to get online,
172	  or to confirm `scripts/sdd-state sync --assume-absent PLAN_FILE` when they
173	  know no remote dossier exists. `diverged` (exit 3): stop and present both
174	  tips and the printed `merge=` command as a question in the Deciding
175	  Together shape; never merge on your own. `busy` (exit 3): retry `sync`
176	  once after the other session's writes settle, then ask.
177	  `foreign` (exit 2): the branch at the state name is not a hyperpowers
178	  state branch; stop, relay the stderr remedy (set git config
179	  `hyperpowers.stateBranch` to another name), and ask.
180	  `remote-finished plans=<ws,...>` (exit 3): another host finished a plan
181	  whose workspace on this host holds ledger lines that are not on the
182	  branch. When the list names this plan's workspace, stop, show your human
183	  partner the lines this host added (the ledger's tail beyond the last
184	  snapshot), and ask whether to snapshot them (then `sync` again, which
185	  reports `diverged` for their merge) or to discard them. A workspace in
186	  the list that is not this plan's belongs to another plan: report its
187	  name and stop without reading or changing it.
188	  Any other status, `status=error` (exit 2) among them, stops the run until
189	  `sync` succeeds; show its stderr line.
190	- Check for this plan's ledger at `<workspace>/progress.md` — this plan's
191	  workspace only, never a sibling's. The workspace is the plan's identity:
192	  `scripts/sdd-dir` maps a plan to ONE directory whatever path form you
193	  hand it, so a ledger sitting in YOUR workspace is this plan's progress
194	  even when its first line records the path in another form. Compare
195	  repo-relative paths: reduce the plan you were handed to its path relative
196	  to the plan repository's toplevel; a line holding an absolute path matches
197	  when it lies inside this repository and reduces to the same relative path,
198	  or, when it names a path that does not exist on this host (a ledger
199	  written on another machine), when it ends with that relative path. Tasks
200	  with a `Task <N>: complete` line are
201	  DONE — do not re-dispatch them; resume at the first task without one. A
202	  task whose last line is a fix round is mid-loop: resume the loop at the
203	  next round. A task whose last line is its implementer identity is a
204	  dispatch in flight: do not re-dispatch — reconcile that agent first
205	  (list your live children, chase its report), resume it by the recorded
206	  id where your harness allows, and only if it cannot be recovered
207	  dispatch a takeover, recording the takeover's identity beside the
208	  original. The scan only reads: every action it calls for (a resume, a
209	  takeover, the next task) waits until `verify` below has returned `ok`.
210	  A first line that resolves to a genuinely different plan
211	  file means this workspace is corrupt, not that you wandered into a
212	  sibling's: stop and surface it to your human partner rather than
213	  starting fresh on top of another plan's state.
214	- When there is no ledger, create it with its identity as the first line,
215	  `# SDD ledger — plan: <repo-relative plan path>` (never an absolute path:
216	  the ledger travels between hosts), through a no-clobber write so two
217	  sessions that both saw no ledger cannot overwrite each other:
218	  `( set -o noclobber; printf '# SDD ledger — plan: %s\n' <repo-relative plan path> > <workspace>/progress.md )`.
219	  When that write fails because the file now exists, another session
220	  created it first: continue as if you had found it. Whether you created
221	  it or found one, append your session line (with `>>`, never a rewrite):
222	  `session <sid>: mode <current-branch|new-branch|worktree> on <task branch or detached> integrating <integration branch> at <toplevel> started <UTC time>`,
223	  where `<sid>` is `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` on Claude Code and, on a harness
224	  without one, a value you generate once as `$(date -u +%s)-$RANDOM$RANDOM`,
225	  and the mode is what using-git-worktrees reported. Append a fresh session
226	  line whenever you move to a new task branch or detach; your newest line is
227	  your location. When your human partner assigned this session specific
228	  tasks, add `session <sid>: tasks <N,M,...>`. Then, once registered (the
229	  next bullet), run `scripts/sdd-state verify PLAN_FILE` (on a found ledger
230	  this is the check that this host holds the code it relies on): `ok` continues;
231	  anything else stops Setup until `verify` passes again. `code-missing`
232	  names commits the ledger relies on that this checkout lacks, so ask your
233	  human partner to pull the named branch; `wrong-repo` names the repository
